- Mode of delivery: Caesarean section may increase physical and emotional stress, possibly increasing PPD risk compared to normal delivery.  
- Need for Support: Higher need for support may indicate emotional vulnerability or existing depression risk.  
- Fear of pregnancy: Fear or anxiety about pregnancy can increase risk of PPD.  
- Residence: Living in a city may provide better healthcare/support but also more stress; rural areas could have less support but possibly less stress—ambiguous effect.  
- Birth compliancy: Complications during birth likely increase risk of PPD.  
- Major changes or losses during pregnancy: Experiencing major changes or losses can increase emotional stress and risk of PPD.  
- Occupation After Your Latest Childbirth: Occupation may relate to stress and social support; housewives may have different risk profiles than working women or students.

