- Gender of newborn: No obvious causal link to postpartum depression.
- Husband’s monthly income: Higher husband income may reduce financial stress, possibly reducing depression risk.
- Number of household members: Larger household may provide more support, reducing depression risk; smaller households might increase isolation.
- Relationship with the newborn: Poor relationship likely increases postpartum depression risk; good or very good reduces it.
- Major changes or losses during pregnancy: Experiencing major changes or losses is a risk factor for postpartum depression.
- Breastfeed: Breastfeeding may be associated with better bonding and lower depression risk.
- Monthly income before latest pregnancy: Similar to current income, lower or no income could increase depression risk.
- Pregnancy length: Very short or premature pregnancies may increase risk due to complications and stress.

